### Why should bytes not flow through the application server?

Three resources get consumed per upload: heap memory if you buffer, a request thread for the entire upload duration, and local disk if you spool to temp files. A single slow 2 GB upload on a mobile connection can hold a thread for ten minutes.

```java
package com.bank.upload.antipattern;

// WRONG: three scarce resources consumed per upload
public class StreamThroughServer {
    // Resource 1: Heap memory (getBytes loads entire file)
    // Resource 2: Request thread (held for entire transfer)
    // Resource 3: Local disk (temp file spooling)
    // A few hundred concurrent large uploads:
    //   - Heap: OutOfMemoryError
    //   - Threads: thread pool exhausted
    //   - Disk: /tmp fills up
    // The server is doing zero useful computation, just copying bytes.
}
```

### How does a presigned URL work?

S3 generates a URL embedding a signature derived from your secret credentials, the specific bucket and key, the allowed method, and a short expiry.

```java
package com.bank.upload.service;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.presigner.S3Presigner;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.presigner.model.PresignedPutObjectRequest;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.model.PutObjectRequest;
import java.time.Duration;

public class S3PresignService {

    private final S3Presigner presigner;

    public S3PresignService(S3Presigner presigner) {
        this.presigner = presigner;
    }

    public String mintUploadUrl(String bucket, String key, String contentType) {
        PutObjectRequest putRequest = PutObjectRequest.builder()
            .bucket(bucket)
            .key(key)
            .contentType(contentType)
            .build();

        PresignedPutObjectRequest presigned = presigner.presignPutObject(req ->
            req.signatureDuration(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
               .putObjectRequest(putRequest));

        return presigned.url().toString();
        // Client uses this URL to PUT bytes directly to S3
        // URL expires in 5 minutes
    }
}
```

```text
# Flow:
# 1. Client -> API: POST /uploads {filename: "kyc-video.mp4", size: 3000000000}
# 2. API validates, mints presigned URL (5 min expiry)
# 3. Client -> S3: PUT bytes using presigned URL
# 4. API never touches the file content
```

### How does S3 multipart upload handle multi-GB files?

A single PUT is impractical for very large files because a network blip restarts the whole transfer. Multipart splits into parts (10 MB each), uploads in parallel, and a failed part retries alone.

```java
package com.bank.upload.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class MultipartUploadFlow {

    public void uploadLargeFile(String bucket, String key, long totalSize) {
        // Step 1: Initiate multipart upload
        String uploadId = initiateMultipart(bucket, key);

        // Step 2: Upload each part (10 MB chunks)
        long partSize = 10_000_000; // 10 MB
        int partCount = (int) Math.ceil((double) totalSize / partSize);
        List<PartETag> completedParts = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 1; i <= partCount; i++) {
            // Get presigned URL for this part
            String partUrl = presignPart(bucket, key, uploadId, i);
            // Client uploads this chunk using the URL
            // On success, record ETag
            completedParts.add(new PartETag(i, "etag-" + i));
            // On failure: retry just this part, not the whole file
        }

        // Step 3: Complete multipart
        completeMultipart(bucket, key, uploadId, completedParts);
    }

    private String initiateMultipart(String b, String k) { return "upload-id"; }
    private String presignPart(String b, String k, String u, int p) { return ""; }
    private void completeMultipart(String b, String k, String u, List<PartETag> p) { }
}
```

### Why must you validate magic bytes, not the extension?

The file extension and declared Content-Type are attacker-controlled strings. An attacker renames `malware.exe` to `invoice.pdf`.

```java
package com.bank.upload.validation;

public class MagicBytesValidator {

    // WRONG: trusting the extension
    public boolean validateByExtension(String filename) {
        return filename.endsWith(".pdf"); // attacker renames .exe to .pdf
    }

    // CORRECT: checking the actual file header
    public boolean validateByMagicBytes(byte[] header) {
        // PDF: starts with %PDF (25 50 44 46)
        if (header[0] == 0x25 && header[1] == 0x50
            && header[2] == 0x44 && header[3] == 0x46) {
            return true;
        }
        // JPEG: starts with FF D8 FF
        if (header[0] == (byte) 0xFF && header[1] == (byte) 0xD8
            && header[2] == (byte) 0xFF) {
            return true;
        }
        // PNG: starts with 89 50 4E 47
        if (header[0] == (byte) 0x89 && header[1] == 0x50
            && header[2] == 0x4E && header[3] == 0x47) {
            return true;
        }
        return false; // reject unknown format
    }
}
```

### How do you enforce size limits?

Enforce a hard maximum before issuing the presigned URL. For multipart, cap total size.

```java
package com.bank.upload.validation;

public class SizeLimitEnforcer {

    private static final long MAX_FILE_SIZE = 500_000_000L; // 500 MB

    public void validateSize(long declaredSize) {
        if (declaredSize > MAX_FILE_SIZE) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "File too large: " + declaredSize + " bytes. Max: " + MAX_FILE_SIZE);
        }
    }

    // Also set content-length-range on presigned URL conditions
    // so S3 rejects uploads exceeding the declared size
}
```

### How does virus scanning work?

Uploaded content lands in a quarantine bucket. An async scanner checks it. Clean files are promoted; infected files are deleted.

```java
package com.bank.upload.security;

public class VirusScanWorker {

    public void scanFile(String quarantineKey) {
        boolean clean = scanWithClamAV(quarantineKey);
        if (clean) {
            moveToUsableBucket(quarantineKey);
            System.out.println("File clean: " + quarantineKey);
        } else {
            deleteInfectedFile(quarantineKey);
            notifyUser("Your file was rejected due to a security concern.");
        }
    }

    private boolean scanWithClamAV(String key) { return true; }
    private void moveToUsableBucket(String key) { }
    private void deleteInfectedFile(String key) { }
    private void notifyUser(String msg) { }
}
```

### What are the security rules for presigned URLs?

Short-lived (5 minutes), scoped to one key and one method, with a content-length-range condition.

```java
package com.bank.upload.security;

public class PresignedUrlSecurity {
    // Rules:
    // 1. Expiry: 5 minutes (a leaked URL expires quickly)
    // 2. Scoped to one key: /uploads/RAJESH/abc123/file.pdf
    // 3. Scoped to one method: PUT only (cannot GET other files)
    // 4. Content-length-range: [0, 500MB] (client cannot upload 50 GB)
    // 5. Content-type condition: application/pdf (client cannot upload .exe)
}
```

